  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 50-year-old man with a history of prediabetes and hyperlipidemia presents with exertional chest pain in the upper left chest and dyspnea associated with exercise. His ASCVD risk score is 1.2% and his EKG shows 1st degree AV block. He had a negative stress test. His family history is notable for early-onset CAD, as his father had an MI at age 55. What would be the next step in evaluating for cardiac causes for this patient's chest pain?",
    "recommendations": "Even with a negative stress test, if clinical suspicion for obstructive CAD remains high due to the typical nature of the exertional symptoms, further testing can be considered. A coronary calcium score can help with risk stratification and decisions about statin therapy; given his family history of early-onset CAD, ordering lipoprotein(a) is also warranted for further risk stratification. For direct evaluation of the coronary arteries, a coronary CTA could be an appropriate next step. An echocardiogram is also reasonable to rule out structural heart disease. Given the concerning symptom profile and family history, an urgent referral to Cardiology is recommended to guide further workup and management."
  },
